@** The application. We have a scene that knows how to read a USD stage and a
renderer that knows how to turn triangles into pixels. What remains is the
program that a person actually sees: a window with a menu bar, a tree of prims
on the left, an inspector on the right, the ray-traced image in the middle and
a status line at the bottom. This part builds that program with SDL3, which
gives us a window, input events and a simple 2D renderer, and Dear ImGui, an
``immediate mode'' user interface library in which the whole interface is
described anew in every frame by ordinary function calls.

The immediate-mode style shapes the code that follows. There are no widget
objects to create, connect and destroy; instead, each frame the application
asks ImGui to draw, say, a menu item, and ImGui answers whether it was clicked.
The state of the interface therefore lives in a handful of plain member
variables of one class, [[App]], and every panel is a member function that
reads them and changes them.

@* The main program. The entry point is as short as it can be: it creates the
application, initializes it with the command-line arguments, and runs it until
the window is closed. Everything interesting happens inside [[App]].

<<UsdOptixViewer/src/main.cpp>>=
// USD OptiX Viewer: loads a USD stage, shows its prim hierarchy and
// properties, and ray traces its geometry with OptiX.
//
//   UsdOptixViewer [file.usd]
#include "App.h"

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    App app;
    if (!app.init(argc, argv))
        return 1;
    app.run();
    return 0;
}
@* The application class. The header \lpfile{App.h} declares the class. Its
public face is tiny: initialization, the main loop, and one function,
[[requestOpen]], that other threads may call. All the rest is private, and
falls into two groups: the functions that draw the parts of the interface or
act on the scene, and the state they share.

<<UsdOptixViewer/src/App.h>>=
