Scene m_scene;
OptixRenderer m_renderer;
bool m_rendererOk = false;
bool m_needsRender = true;
@ The camera is an \emph{orbit} camera: it always looks at a target point,
from a given distance, along a direction given by two angles, the yaw around
the up axis and the pitch above the horizon. The field of view is vertical and
measured in degrees. Alternatively, the view may come from one of the stage's
own cameras; [[m_camera]] then holds its index, and the orbit parameters are
ignored until the user moves the view. The light direction and the four
switches of the View menu complete the parameters of the image.

<<Camera and display options of the application>>=
// orbit camera
float m_target[3] = {0, 0, 0};
float m_distance = 5.0f, m_yaw = 0.6f, m_pitch = 0.4f, m_fovY = 45.0f;
int m_camera = -1;  // USD camera we look through (index into Scene::cameras()), -1 for the orbit camera
float m_light[3] = {0.4f, 0.8f, 0.45f};
bool m_shadows = true;
bool m_mipmaps = true;
bool m_normalMaps = true;
bool m_reflections = true;
@ The selection is the USD path of a prim, and the empty path means that
nothing is selected. A path is the natural name for a prim: it is what the
stage hands out, what the command line and the inspector show, and it stays
meaningful whatever the scene and the renderer derive from the stage. When a
prim is selected by clicking on the image, the tree must open all its
ancestors so that it becomes visible; [[m_reveal]] holds their paths until the
tree has been drawn once. The filter of the tree keeps the text being typed,
the text for which the filter was last computed, and the resulting set of
paths to show. Both sets are hash sets of paths, with the hash function that
[[SdfPath]] provides.

