@ Two constants govern the whole interface. The renderer is progressive: it
adds one sample per pixel and frame and averages them. After [[kMaxSamples]]
samples the image has converged well enough, antialiased edges and glossy
reflections included, and we stop spending the GPU's time on it. At the
display's refresh rate of 60 frames per second, 256 samples take a little over
four seconds.

The panels are not meant to be moved, resized or folded by the user; their
places are computed every frame from the size of the window
(<<Lay out the panels and handle shortcuts>>). [[kPanelFlags]] says so, and also
asks ImGui not to save their positions in a settings file.

<<Constants of the application>>=
constexpr unsigned int kMaxSamples = 256;  // progressive samples per pixel

constexpr ImGuiWindowFlags kPanelFlags =
    ImGuiWindowFlags_NoMove | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoResize | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoCollapse | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoSavedSettings;
@* Creation and destruction. Initialization proceeds in four steps: the
window, the user interface library, the command line, and the ray tracer
(after the XR output, if the command line asks for one), and finally the
file, prim and camera that the command line names. A failure of SDL is fatal, but a failure of OptiX is
not; it is reported in a popup and the viewport stays empty.

<<Initialize the application>>=
bool App::init(int argc, char** argv)
{
    <<Create the window and the SDL renderer>>

    <<Set up Dear ImGui>>

    <<Parse the command line>>

    <<Start the XR output and the OptiX renderer>>

    <<Open the requested file and select the requested prim>>
    <<Look through the requested camera>>
    return true;
}
@ On a high-density display, a window of 1600 by 960 \emph{logical} units may
cover twice as many physical pixels. SDL reports the ratio the desktop uses
for its own text as the display's \emph{content scale}; we size the window by
it so that it occupies the same fraction of the screen everywhere, and ask for
a framebuffer at full pixel density with
[[SDL_WINDOW_HIGH_PIXEL_DENSITY]].

For drawing we use SDL's own 2D renderer rather than OpenGL. The interface is
just textured triangles, which [[SDL_Renderer]] draws with whatever back end
the platform offers, and our one large image---the ray-traced view---arrives
as an ordinary array of pixels in main memory (the renderer copies it there
anyway, because it also reads back the mesh identifiers used for picking). A
streaming SDL texture is the simplest home for such an array; sharing a buffer
between CUDA and OpenGL would avoid one copy per frame, but would tie the
program to one graphics API for a saving that does not matter at these
resolutions. Vertical synchronization paces the main loop at the display's
refresh rate, which also paces the progressive rendering.

<<Create the window and the SDL renderer>>=
if (!SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO))
{
    SDL_Log("SDL_Init failed: %s", SDL_GetError());
    return false;
}
const float scale = SDL_GetDisplayContentScale(SDL_GetPrimaryDisplay());
m_window = SDL_CreateWindow("USD OptiX Viewer", int(1600 * scale), int(960 * scale),
                            SDL_WINDOW_RESIZABLE | SDL_WINDOW_HIGH_PIXEL_DENSITY);
if (!m_window)
{
    SDL_Log("SDL_CreateWindow failed: %s", SDL_GetError());
    return false;
}
m_sdlRenderer = SDL_CreateRenderer(m_window, nullptr);
if (!m_sdlRenderer)
{
    SDL_Log("SDL_CreateRenderer failed: %s", SDL_GetError());
    return false;
}
SDL_SetRenderVSync(m_sdlRenderer, 1);
@ ImGui is set up as in its SDL3 example, with three changes. Keyboard
navigation is enabled; the settings file \lpfile{imgui.ini} is disabled
([[IniFilename]] set to null), since our panels have fixed places and there is
nothing worth remembering; and window corners are square, because the panels
tile the window. The same content scale enlarges all sizes and fonts, so that
the interface is as legible on a high-density screen as on an ordinary one.
The two back ends connect ImGui to SDL's events and to SDL's renderer.

<<Set up Dear ImGui>>=
IMGUI_CHECKVERSION();
ImGui::CreateContext();
ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();
io.ConfigFlags |= ImGuiConfigFlags_NavEnableKeyboard;
io.IniFilename = nullptr;
ImGui::StyleColorsDark();
ImGuiStyle& style = ImGui::GetStyle();
style.ScaleAllSizes(scale);
style.FontScaleDpi = scale;
style.WindowRounding = 0.0f;
ImGui_ImplSDL3_InitForSDLRenderer(m_window, m_sdlRenderer);
ImGui_ImplSDLRenderer3_Init(m_sdlRenderer);
@ The command line names at most one file, and may add options. Three of them
switch off a feature of the renderer from the start, exactly as the
corresponding items of the View menu do; they make it easy to compare images
with and without mipmaps, normal maps or traced reflections.

The fourth, \texttt{--select}, selects a prim by its path and frames the
camera on it. It exists mostly for testing: synthetic mouse clicks sent to
the window by \texttt{xdotool} do not reach SDL on the development machine's
display, so the way to check the selection, the tree and the inspector from a
script is to select a prim from the command line and take a screenshot. For the same reason
\texttt{--camera} looks through a USD camera given by its path, as the Camera
menu does.

The remaining options concern the XR output, described at the end of this
part. \texttt{--xr} sends the view to the active OpenXR runtime, and
\texttt{--cloudxr} first starts NVIDIA's CloudXR runtime inside the program,
from the SDK directory that \texttt{--cloudxr-sdk} names, with the device
profile of \texttt{--cloudxr-profile}. The last three set the samples per
pixel and frame, the eye resolution as a fraction of the headset's
recommendation, and the mirror of the left eye; the numbers are clamped to
sensible ranges.

<<Parse the command line>>=
// UsdOptixViewer [file.usd] [--select /Prim/Path] [--camera /Camera/Path] [--no-mipmaps] [--no-normal-maps]
//                [--no-reflections] [--xr] [--cloudxr] [--cloudxr-sdk DIR] [--cloudxr-profile NAME]
//                [--xr-samples N] [--xr-resolution F] [--xr-mirror]
std::string file, selectPath, cameraPath, cloudXrSdk, cloudXrProfile;
bool xr = false, cloudXr = false;
for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i)
{
    const std::string arg = argv[i];
    if (arg == "--select" && i + 1 < argc)
        selectPath = argv[++i];
    else if (arg == "--camera" && i + 1 < argc)
        cameraPath = argv[++i];
    else if (arg == "--xr")
        xr = true;
    else if (arg == "--cloudxr")
        cloudXr = true;
    else if (arg == "--cloudxr-sdk" && i + 1 < argc)
        cloudXrSdk = argv[++i];
    else if (arg == "--cloudxr-profile" && i + 1 < argc)
        cloudXrProfile = argv[++i];
    else if (arg == "--xr-samples" && i + 1 < argc)
        m_xrSamples = std::clamp(std::atoi(argv[++i]), 1, 64);
    else if (arg == "--xr-resolution" && i + 1 < argc)
        m_xrResolution = std::clamp(float(std::atof(argv[++i])), 0.1f, 2.0f);
    else if (arg == "--xr-mirror")
        m_xrMirror = true;
    else if (arg == "--no-mipmaps")
        m_mipmaps = false;
    else if (arg == "--no-normal-maps")
        m_normalMaps = false;
    else if (arg == "--no-reflections")
        m_reflections = false;
    else
        file = arg;
}
@ The XR runtime, if one is wanted, starts before the renderer: the CloudXR
service must run before any OpenXR call, and the renderer may later have to
move to the GPU that the runtime picks (see <<Follow the XR session>>). If
OptiX cannot start, the message goes into [[m_error]], which the next frame
shows in a popup (<<Draw the popups>>).

<<Start the XR output and the OptiX renderer>>=
// The XR runtime must exist before the renderer starts, because it may
// choose another GPU (see updateXr).
if (xr || cloudXr)
    startXr(cloudXr, cloudXrSdk, cloudXrProfile);
std::string error;
m_rendererOk = m_renderer.init(error);
if (!m_rendererOk)
    m_error = "OptiX initialization failed: " + error;
@ The prim to select must exist on the stage, which we ask the stage itself,
after checking that the string is a valid path at all (the constructor of
[[SdfPath]] would complain loudly otherwise). Selecting with [[reveal]] set
opens its ancestors in the tree, and [[frameSelection]] points the camera at
it.

<<Open the requested file and select the requested prim>>=
if (!file.empty())
    loadFile(file);
if (!selectPath.empty() && m_scene.loaded() && SdfPath::IsValidPathString(selectPath))
{
    const SdfPath path(selectPath);
    if (m_scene.stage()->GetPrimAtPath(path))
    {
        select(path, true);
        frameSelection();
    }
}
@ The camera comes last, because selecting a prim frames it with the orbit
camera. A path that names no camera of the stage is reported in the error
popup.

<<Look through the requested camera>>=
if (!cameraPath.empty())
{
    const std::vector<SceneCamera>& cameras = m_scene.cameras();
    const auto it = std::find_if(cameras.begin(), cameras.end(),
                                 [&](const SceneCamera& c) { return c.path == cameraPath; });
    if (it != cameras.end())
        lookThrough(int(it - cameras.begin()));
    else
        m_error = "No camera at " + cameraPath;
}
@ Destruction undoes initialization in reverse. ImGui is shut down only if it
was created, because [[init]] may have failed before reaching that point. The
scene and the OptiX renderer are members and clean up after themselves when
the object's lifetime ends.

<<Destroy the application>>=
App::~App()
{
    if (ImGui::GetCurrentContext())
    {
        ImGui_ImplSDLRenderer3_Shutdown();
        ImGui_ImplSDL3_Shutdown();
        ImGui::DestroyContext();
    }
    if (m_texture)
        SDL_DestroyTexture(m_texture);
    if (m_mirrorTexture)
        SDL_DestroyTexture(m_mirrorTexture);
    if (m_sdlRenderer)
        SDL_DestroyRenderer(m_sdlRenderer);
    if (m_window)
        SDL_DestroyWindow(m_window);
    SDL_Quit();
}
@* Opening files. A file may be requested in four ways: on the command line,
through the file dialog, by typing a path in the File menu, or by dropping it
on the window. All but the first go through [[requestOpen]], which merely
records the path; the main loop loads it at a quiet moment, between two
frames. This matters for the file dialog: SDL shows it asynchronously and may
call us back on another thread, and loading a stage there would race with the
drawing of the interface. A mutex makes the hand-over safe. If two requests
arrive in the same frame, the later one wins, which is what a user who changes
their mind expects.

<<Request a file to be opened>>=
void App::requestOpen(const std::string& path)
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_pendingMutex);
    m_pendingOpen = path;
}
@ Loading forgets everything that belonged to the previous stage: the camera
we were looking through, the selection, the prims to reveal and the filter
of the tree. If the scene
cannot open the file, its message is shown in the error popup. Otherwise the
path is copied into the text field of the File menu (so that it can be edited
and reloaded), the window title names the file, the scene's meshes, materials
and textures are sent to the renderer, and the camera frames the whole stage.

<<Load a USD file into the scene and the renderer>>=
void App::loadFile(const std::string& path)
{
    std::string error;
    m_camera = -1;  // camera indices refer to the previous stage
    m_selected = SdfPath();
    m_reveal.clear();
    m_appliedFilter.clear();
    m_filter[0] = '\0';
    if (!m_scene.load(path, error))
    {
        m_error = error;
        return;
    }
    SDL_strlcpy(m_pathInput, path.c_str(), sizeof(m_pathInput));
    SDL_SetWindowTitle(m_window, ("USD OptiX Viewer - " + path).c_str());
    if (m_rendererOk)
    {
        m_renderer.setScene(m_scene);
        m_renderer.setSelectedMesh(kNoMesh);
    }
    frameBounds(m_scene.boundsMin(), m_scene.boundsMax());
}
@ The dialog is SDL's portable one; it uses the desktop's native dialog where
there is one. Its callback receives a list of chosen files, an empty list if
the user cancelled, or a null pointer if something went wrong. The filter
list offers the four USD extensions first.

<<Show the file dialog>>=
void App::openFileDialog()
{
    static const SDL_DialogFileFilter filters[] = {{"USD files", "usd;usda;usdc;usdz"}, {"All files", "*"}};
    SDL_ShowOpenFileDialog(
        [](void* userdata, const char* const* files, int) {
            if (files && files[0])
                static_cast<App*>(userdata)->requestOpen(files[0]);
            else if (!files)
                SDL_Log("File dialog error: %s", SDL_GetError());
        },
        this, m_window, filters, 2, nullptr, false);
}
@* The main loop. Each turn of the loop handles the pending events, loads a
requested file if there is one, and then draws one frame. The pending path is
taken out under the lock by swapping it with an empty string, and loaded
after the lock has been released, so that the dialog's thread never waits for
a stage to load. Then the XR output gets its turn (<<Follow the XR session>>),
before the minimized test, so that the headset keeps receiving images while
the window is minimized. While the window is minimized we draw nothing and
sleep a little, rather than spin.

Drawing a frame is ImGui's usual ritual: start a new frame in both back ends
and in ImGui, describe the whole interface (<<Lay out the panels and handle shortcuts>>),
let ImGui turn the description into triangles, and have SDL draw
them. On a high-density display, ImGui works in logical units and SDL must
scale its drawing by the framebuffer scale to match.

<<The main loop of the application>>=
void App::run()
{
    while (m_running)
    {
        SDL_Event event;
        while (SDL_PollEvent(&event))
            processEvent(event);

        std::string pending;
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_pendingMutex);
            pending.swap(m_pendingOpen);
        }
        if (!pending.empty())
            loadFile(pending);

        updateXr();  // keeps streaming while the window is minimized
        if (SDL_GetWindowFlags(m_window) & SDL_WINDOW_MINIMIZED)
        {
            SDL_Delay(10);
            continue;
        }

        ImGui_ImplSDLRenderer3_NewFrame();
        ImGui_ImplSDL3_NewFrame();
        ImGui::NewFrame();
        drawUi();
        ImGui::Render();

        const ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();
        SDL_SetRenderScale(m_sdlRenderer, io.DisplayFramebufferScale.x, io.DisplayFramebufferScale.y);
        SDL_SetRenderDrawColorFloat(m_sdlRenderer, 0.08f, 0.08f, 0.09f, 1.0f);
        SDL_RenderClear(m_sdlRenderer);
        ImGui_ImplSDLRenderer3_RenderDrawData(ImGui::GetDrawData(), m_sdlRenderer);
        SDL_RenderPresent(m_sdlRenderer);
    }
}
@ Every event goes first to ImGui, which keeps its own record of the mouse
and the keyboard. Only three events concern the application itself: quitting,
closing the window, and a file dropped on it. A dropped file takes the same
road as a file chosen in the dialog.

<<Handle an SDL event>>=
void App::processEvent(const SDL_Event& event)
{
    ImGui_ImplSDL3_ProcessEvent(&event);
    switch (event.type)
    {
    case SDL_EVENT_QUIT:
        m_running = false;
        break;
    case SDL_EVENT_WINDOW_CLOSE_REQUESTED:
        if (event.window.windowID == SDL_GetWindowID(m_window))
            m_running = false;
        break;
    case SDL_EVENT_DROP_FILE:
        if (event.drop.data)
            requestOpen(event.drop.data);
        break;
    default:
        break;
    }
}
@* The layout. The window is divided into fixed regions. The menu bar sits on
top, and ImGui reports the rest of the window as the main viewport's
\emph{work area}, whose top edge [[WorkPos.y]] is just below the menu. A status
bar one line high takes the bottom. Between them, the hierarchy takes 22\% of
the width (but at least 260 units), the inspector 28\% (at least 320), and the
viewport the rest. Each panel's position and size are set just before it is
drawn.

<<Lay out the panels and handle shortcuts>>=
void App::drawUi()
{
    drawMenuBar();

    <<Compute the regions of the window>>

    <<Place and draw the four panels>>

    drawPopups();

    <<Handle the keyboard shortcuts>>
}
@
<<Compute the regions of the window>>=
const ImGuiViewport* vp = ImGui::GetMainViewport();
const float statusH = ImGui::GetFrameHeight() + ImGui::GetStyle().WindowPadding.y;
const float top = vp->WorkPos.y, bottom = vp->Pos.y + vp->Size.y - statusH;
const float leftW = std::max(260.0f, vp->Size.x * 0.22f), rightW = std::max(320.0f, vp->Size.x * 0.28f);
@
<<Place and draw the four panels>>=
ImGui::SetNextWindowPos(ImVec2(vp->Pos.x, top));
ImGui::SetNextWindowSize(ImVec2(leftW, bottom - top));
drawHierarchy();

ImGui::SetNextWindowPos(ImVec2(vp->Pos.x + vp->Size.x - rightW, top));
ImGui::SetNextWindowSize(ImVec2(rightW, bottom - top));
drawInspector();

ImGui::SetNextWindowPos(ImVec2(vp->Pos.x + leftW, top));
ImGui::SetNextWindowSize(ImVec2(vp->Size.x - leftW - rightW, bottom - top));
drawViewport();

ImGui::SetNextWindowPos(ImVec2(vp->Pos.x, bottom));
ImGui::SetNextWindowSize(ImVec2(vp->Size.x, statusH));
drawStatusBar();
@ Three keys act on the whole application: \textsf{F} frames the selection
(or the whole stage when nothing is selected), \textsf{Esc} clears the
selection, and \textsf{Ctrl+O} opens the file dialog. They must not fire while
the user is typing in a text field---typing ``foo'' in the filter of the tree
should not move the camera---so ImGui's [[WantTextInput]] guards them.

<<Handle the keyboard shortcuts>>=
if (!ImGui::GetIO().WantTextInput)
{
    if (ImGui::IsKeyPressed(ImGuiKey_F))
        frameSelection();
    if (ImGui::IsKeyPressed(ImGuiKey_Escape))
        select(SdfPath(), false);
    if (ImGui::IsKeyDown(ImGuiMod_Ctrl) && ImGui::IsKeyPressed(ImGuiKey_O))
        openFileDialog();
}
@* Menus. The menu bar has five menus.

<<Draw the menu bar>>=
