@ The About box cannot be opened from inside the menu, because ImGui's popups
are identified by the stack of windows that opens them; the menu only raises a
flag, and <<Draw the popups>> opens the box at the top level.

<<The Help menu>>=
if (ImGui::BeginMenu("Help"))
{
    if (ImGui::MenuItem("About"))
        m_showAbout = true;
    ImGui::EndMenu();
}
@* The hierarchy. The left panel shows the prims of the stage as a tree. A
USD stage already is a scene graph: every prim knows its name, its type, its
parent and its children, so the tree is read straight from the stage, with no
copy of the hierarchy to build and keep in step. The tree is drawn by a
recursive function, starting from the children of the pseudo-root, the
invisible prim at the top of every USD stage.

Children are listed with the predicate [[UsdTraverseInstanceProxies]]. The
prims below an instance live in a shared prototype and are not children of
the instance in the ordinary sense; the predicate presents them as
\emph{instance proxies}, prims with paths of their own under the instance.
That is how the scene sees them too, since it draws the meshes of instances,
so a mesh picked in the image can always be found in the tree.

Above the tree, a text field filters the prims by name. The filter is
recomputed only when its text changes (<<Recompute the hierarchy filter>>), not
in every frame.

<<Draw the hierarchy panel>>=
void App::drawHierarchy()
{
    ImGui::Begin("Hierarchy", nullptr, kPanelFlags);
    if (!m_scene.loaded())
    {
        ImGui::TextWrapped("No stage loaded.\n\nUse File > Open, drop a .usd/.usda/.usdc/.usdz file on the "
                           "window, or pass a file on the command line.");
        ImGui::End();
        return;
    }
    ImGui::SetNextItemWidth(-FLT_MIN);
    ImGui::InputTextWithHint("##filter", "Filter by name", m_filter, sizeof(m_filter));
    updateFilter();
    ImGui::Separator();
    ImGui::BeginChild("tree");
    for (const UsdPrim& child : m_scene.stage()->GetPseudoRoot().GetFilteredChildren(UsdTraverseInstanceProxies()))
        drawHierarchyNode(child);
    m_revealSelection = false;
    ImGui::EndChild();
    ImGui::End();
}
@ A filtered tree must show not only the prims whose names match, but also
every ancestor of each match; otherwise the matches would have no branch to
hang from. Unlike the drawing of the tree, the filter must look at every
prim, open or not, so it walks the whole stage with a [[UsdPrimRange]], again
including instance proxies. For each matching prim we walk up its path,
inserting each prefix in the set; a path's parent is just the path with its
last element removed, so no lookup on the stage is needed. The walk stops at
the absolute root path, which stands for the pseudo-root and is never drawn,
or as soon as an insertion fails: a path already in the set has had its own
ancestors inserted before, by the same walk, so there is no point in climbing
further. The total work is therefore proportional to the number of prims,
however many of them match.

<<Recompute the hierarchy filter>>=
void App::updateFilter()
{
    if (m_appliedFilter == m_filter)
        return;
    m_appliedFilter = m_filter;
    m_visibleByFilter.clear();
    if (m_appliedFilter.empty())
        return;
    for (const UsdPrim& prim : UsdPrimRange::Stage(m_scene.stage(), UsdTraverseInstanceProxies()))
    {
        if (!containsCaseInsensitive(prim.GetName().GetString(), m_appliedFilter))
            continue;
        for (SdfPath a = prim.GetPath(); a != SdfPath::AbsoluteRootPath() && m_visibleByFilter.insert(a).second;)
            a = a.GetParentPath();
    }
}
@ Names are compared without regard to case, which is what one expects of a
search field. The standard library has no such function, but
[[std::search]] with a predicate that compares lowered characters does the
job. (The cast to [[unsigned char]] keeps [[std::tolower]] defined for bytes
above 127.)

<<Search a name ignoring case>>=
bool containsCaseInsensitive(const std::string& text, const std::string& pattern)
{
    return std::search(text.begin(), text.end(), pattern.begin(), pattern.end(), [](char a, char b) {
               return std::tolower(static_cast<unsigned char>(a)) == std::tolower(static_cast<unsigned char>(b));
           }) != text.end();
}
@ Drawing a node. A node is skipped if a filter is active and its path is not
in the filter's set. Otherwise we ask the stage for the prim's children; the
answer is a lazy range, so finding out whether there are any costs one step.
Then we compute the flags of ImGui's tree node: it opens only with its arrow
or a double click (so that a single click on the label selects), it spans the
width of the panel, it is a leaf if it has no children, and it is highlighted
if it is the selection.

The recursion descends only into open nodes. A large stage may have hundreds
of thousands of prims, but a person looks at a few dozen rows of the tree at
a time; a closed node costs the same whatever lies beneath it, so drawing the
tree takes time in proportion to what is shown, not to the size of the stage.

<<Draw one node of the hierarchy>>=
// The tree is drawn straight from the stage: USD already holds the hierarchy,
// names and types. Only open nodes visit their children.
void App::drawHierarchyNode(const UsdPrim& prim)
{
    const SdfPath& path = prim.GetPath();
    if (!m_appliedFilter.empty() && !m_visibleByFilter.contains(path))
        return;
    const UsdPrimSiblingRange children = prim.GetFilteredChildren(UsdTraverseInstanceProxies());
    const bool leaf = children.empty();

    <<Choose the flags of the tree node>>

    <<Draw the tree node and react to clicks>>

    if (open && !leaf)
    {
        for (const UsdPrim& child : children)
            drawHierarchyNode(child);
        ImGui::TreePop();
    }
}
@ Leaves get [[ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_NoTreePushOnOpen]], so that they need no
matching [[TreePop]]; that is why the recursion above pops only nodes with
children.

Two situations open a node from the program rather than by the user's
hand. When the selection must be revealed, every ancestor of the selected
prim is forced open, once. When a filter is active, every visible node is
opened when it first appears, so that all matches can be seen; the condition
[[ImGuiCond_Appearing]] still lets the user fold a branch afterwards.

<<Choose the flags of the tree node>>=
ImGuiTreeNodeFlags flags = ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_OpenOnArrow | ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_OpenOnDoubleClick |
                           ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_SpanAvailWidth;
if (leaf)
    flags |= ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_Leaf | ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_NoTreePushOnOpen;
if (path == m_selected)
    flags |= ImGuiTreeNodeFlags_Selected;
if (m_revealSelection && m_reveal.contains(path))
    ImGui::SetNextItemOpen(true);
if (!m_appliedFilter.empty())
    ImGui::SetNextItemOpen(true, ImGuiCond_Appearing);
@ Prims that produced triangles for the renderer, those for which the scene
has a mesh, are drawn in light blue, so that the geometry stands out among
transforms, scopes and materials. ImGui identifies tree nodes by an ID; prim
names are not unique (many prims are called ``Mesh''), but paths are, so we
pass the path's text as the ID and the name only as the label.

A click on the label selects the prim, while a click on the arrow merely
opens or closes the node; the function [[IsItemToggledOpen]] tells the two
apart. When the selection is being revealed, the function [[SetScrollHereY]]
scrolls the panel to the selected node. The type of the prim follows its name,
in grey.

<<Draw the tree node and react to clicks>>=
const bool hasMesh = m_scene.meshIndex(path) >= 0;
if (hasMesh)
    ImGui::PushStyleColor(ImGuiCol_Text, ImVec4(0.6f, 0.85f, 1.0f, 1.0f));
const bool open = ImGui::TreeNodeEx(path.GetText(), flags, "%s", prim.GetName().GetText());
if (hasMesh)
    ImGui::PopStyleColor();
if (ImGui::IsItemClicked() && !ImGui::IsItemToggledOpen())
    select(path, false);
if (m_revealSelection && path == m_selected)
    ImGui::SetScrollHereY();
if (!prim.GetTypeName().IsEmpty())
{
    ImGui::SameLine();
    ImGui::TextDisabled("%s", prim.GetTypeName().GetText());
}
@ Selecting a prim informs the renderer, which tints the selected mesh if the
prim has one, and restarts the rendering. The renderer knows meshes by their
mesh id, which is the mesh's index in the scene, and [[meshIndex]] finds it
for a path. A selection made in the tree needs no revealing; one made by
clicking on the image does, so in that case we collect the paths of the
prim's ancestors, up to but excluding the absolute root, and raise
[[m_revealSelection]]. The flag is lowered again at the end of the next
drawing of the tree (<<Draw the hierarchy panel>>), so the forced opening and
scrolling happen exactly once, and the user is free to fold the branches
again.

<<Select a prim>>=
void App::select(const SdfPath& path, bool reveal)
{
    m_selected = path;
    const int mesh = m_scene.meshIndex(path);
    m_renderer.setSelectedMesh(mesh >= 0 ? unsigned(mesh) : kNoMesh);
    m_needsRender = true;
    m_reveal.clear();
    if (reveal && !path.IsEmpty())
    {
        for (SdfPath a = path.GetParentPath(); a != SdfPath::AbsoluteRootPath() && !a.IsEmpty(); a = a.GetParentPath())
            m_reveal.insert(a);
        m_revealSelection = true;
    }
}
@* The inspector. The right panel describes the selected prim; when nothing
is selected, it describes the stage. For a prim it has three or four
sections: the prim's identity, what the viewer made of it---a camera, or the
geometry and material that the renderer draws---if anything, and a table of
all its properties. The prim comes from the stage by its path; an empty path
asks for no prim at all.

<<Draw the inspector>>=
void App::drawInspector()
{
    ImGui::Begin("Inspector", nullptr, kPanelFlags);
    const UsdPrim prim = m_selected.IsEmpty() ? UsdPrim() : m_scene.stage()->GetPrimAtPath(m_selected);
    if (!prim)
    {
        <<Describe the stage when nothing is selected>>
        ImGui::End();
        return;
    }

    <<Describe the identity of the prim>>

    <<Describe the camera and offer to look through it>>

    <<Describe the rendered geometry and its material>>

    <<List the properties of the prim>>
    ImGui::End();
}
@ The stage summary gives the file, the stage's up axis and units, its time
range if it is animated, its default prim, and how many materials, textures
and cameras the scene found. Any warnings the scene collected while reading materials and
textures (an unsupported shader, a missing file, a translucent material drawn
opaque) are listed in orange; they explain why an image may differ from what
another renderer would show.

<<Describe the stage when nothing is selected>>=
if (m_scene.loaded())
{
    const UsdStageRefPtr& stage = m_scene.stage();
    ImGui::SeparatorText("Stage");
    ImGui::TextWrapped("%s", m_scene.filePath().c_str());
    ImGui::Text("Up axis: %s", m_scene.zUp() ? "Z" : "Y");
    ImGui::Text("Meters per unit: %g", UsdGeomGetStageMetersPerUnit(stage));
    if (stage->HasAuthoredTimeCodeRange())
        ImGui::Text("Time codes: %g - %g", stage->GetStartTimeCode(), stage->GetEndTimeCode());
    if (SdfPrimSpecHandle def = stage->GetRootLayer()->GetPrimAtPath(stage->GetDefaultPrim().GetPath()))
        ImGui::Text("Default prim: %s", def->GetName().c_str());
    ImGui::Text("Materials: %zu   Textures: %zu   Cameras: %zu", m_scene.materials().size(),
                m_scene.textures().size(), m_scene.cameras().size());
    if (!m_scene.warnings().empty())
    {
        ImGui::SeparatorText("Warnings");
        for (const std::string& w : m_scene.warnings())
            ImGui::TextColored(ImVec4(1.0f, 0.75f, 0.3f, 1.0f), "%s", w.c_str());
    }
    ImGui::Spacing();
    ImGui::TextDisabled("Select a prim in the hierarchy or click on the viewport.");
}
@ The identity of a prim is shown as a two-column table of key and value,
filled by a small local function [[row]]. Some rows appear only when they
say something: the \emph{kind} of a model, instancing, references, applied API
schemas. The specifier (def, over or class) is printed by OpenUSD as, for
instance, ``SdfSpecifierDef''; we strip the prefix.

<<Describe the identity of the prim>>=
ImGui::SeparatorText("Prim");
ImGui::TextWrapped("%s", prim.GetPath().GetText());
if (ImGui::BeginTable("prim", 2, ImGuiTableFlags_SizingStretchProp))
{
    auto row = [](const char* key, const std::string& value) {
        ImGui::TableNextRow();
        ImGui::TableNextColumn();
        ImGui::TextDisabled("%s", key);
        ImGui::TableNextColumn();
        ImGui::TextWrapped("%s", value.c_str());
    };
    row("Type", prim.GetTypeName().IsEmpty() ? "(typeless)" : prim.GetTypeName().GetString());
    row("Specifier", TfStringReplace(TfStringify(prim.GetSpecifier()), "SdfSpecifier", ""));
    TfToken kind;
    if (UsdModelAPI(prim).GetKind(&kind) && !kind.IsEmpty())
        row("Kind", kind.GetString());
    row("Active", prim.IsActive() ? "yes" : "no");
    if (prim.IsInstance() || prim.IsInstanceProxy())
        row("Instancing", prim.IsInstance() ? "instance" : "instance proxy");
    if (prim.HasAuthoredReferences())
        row("Composition", "has references");
    const std::vector<std::string> schemas = [&] {
        std::vector<std::string> out;
        for (const TfToken& t : prim.GetAppliedSchemas())
            out.push_back(t.GetString());
        return out;
    }();
    if (!schemas.empty())
        row("API schemas", TfStringJoin(schemas, ", "));
    ImGui::EndTable();
}
@ A camera prim shows what the viewer understood of it: the projection, the
vertical field of view or the size of an orthographic view, and the clipping
range. The raw lens attributes are in the property table below. The button
looks through the camera; it is disabled, and says so, when we already do.
Whether the prim is a camera the scene understood is answered by
[[cameraIndex]], which gives its index among the scene's cameras, or $-1$.

<<Describe the camera and offer to look through it>>=
if (const int camera = m_scene.cameraIndex(m_selected); camera >= 0)
{
    const SceneCamera& c = m_scene.cameras()[camera];
    ImGui::SeparatorText("Camera");
    if (c.orthographic)
        ImGui::Text("Orthographic, %g x %g units", 2.0f * c.halfWidth, 2.0f * c.halfHeight);
    else
        ImGui::Text("Perspective, vertical field of view %.1f deg", c.fovYDeg);
    ImGui::Text("Clipping range: %g - %g", c.clipNear, c.clipFar);
    const bool active = m_camera == camera;
    ImGui::BeginDisabled(active);
    if (ImGui::Button(active ? "Looking through this camera" : "Look through"))
        lookThrough(camera);
    ImGui::EndDisabled();
}
@ The next section exists only for prims that the scene turned into
triangles, that is, prims for which [[meshIndex]] finds a [[RenderMesh]]. It
shows what the \emph{renderer} sees, after the scene has resolved the bound
material, which is often more useful than the raw attributes when an image
looks wrong: the size of the mesh, the material's path, and each of its
inputs in turn. A button frames the prim.

The size is given in triangles and vertices, and with the number of triangle
corners the vertices came from. The scene starts with one vertex per corner,
since a corner may have a normal and texture coordinates of its own, and then
welds corners that agree in everything; the two numbers show how much
sharing the mesh allowed. A smooth, seamless mesh comes down to about one
vertex per two triangles, while a mesh with flat shading or many texture seams
keeps closer to three.

<<Describe the rendered geometry and its material>>=
if (const int meshIndex = m_scene.meshIndex(m_selected); meshIndex >= 0)
{
    const RenderMesh* mesh = &m_scene.meshes()[meshIndex];
    ImGui::SeparatorText("Rendered geometry");
    ImGui::Text("Triangles: %zu   Vertices: %zu (from %zu corners)", mesh->triangleCount(), mesh->vertexCount(),
                mesh->corners);
    const std::vector<Texture>& textures = m_scene.textures();
    const Material& material = m_scene.materials()[mesh->material];
    ImGui::TextWrapped("Material: %s", material.name.c_str());
    <<Describe the diffuse color or texture>>
    <<Describe the normal map>>
    <<Describe roughness, metallic or specular color, and ior>>
    if (mesh->subsetMaterials)
        ImGui::Text("GeomSubsets with their own material: %u", mesh->subsetMaterials);
    if (ImGui::Button("Frame (F)"))
        frameSelection();
}
@ A diffuse texture is described by its resolved path (which may point inside
a \texttt{.usdz} package), its size, its color space and the primvar that
supplies its texture coordinates. If the prim lacks that primvar the texture
cannot be applied, and we say so. Without a texture, the diffuse color is
shown as a small swatch; the [[ColorEdit3]] widget is used only for display,
with its inputs and picker disabled.

<<Describe the diffuse color or texture>>=
if (material.texture >= 0)
{
    const Texture& t = textures[material.texture];
    ImGui::TextWrapped("Diffuse texture: %s", t.path.c_str());
    ImGui::Text("  %d x %d, %s, uv set '%s'%s", t.width, t.height, t.srgb ? "sRGB" : "raw",
                material.uvSet.c_str(), mesh->uvs.empty() ? " (missing!)" : "");
}
else
{
    float color[3] = {material.color[0], material.color[1], material.color[2]};
    ImGui::ColorEdit3("Diffuse color", color, ImGuiColorEditFlags_NoInputs | ImGuiColorEditFlags_NoPicker);
}
@ For a normal map, the scale and bias matter as much as the image: they
decide how the stored colors become tangent-space vectors, and an asset that
flips the green channel (as many DirectX-style maps do) does it through them.

<<Describe the normal map>>=
if (material.normalTexture >= 0)
{
    const Texture& t = textures[material.normalTexture];
    ImGui::TextWrapped("Normal map: %s", t.path.c_str());
    ImGui::Text("  %d x %d, %s, scale (%g, %g, %g), bias (%g, %g, %g)", t.width, t.height,
                t.srgb ? "sRGB" : "raw", material.normalScale[0], material.normalScale[1],
                material.normalScale[2], material.normalBias[0], material.normalBias[1],
                material.normalBias[2]);
}
@ Roughness and metallic are scalar inputs that may be constants or one
channel of a texture; the local function [[scalarText]] prints either form,
naming the channel by indexing the string \texttt{"rgba"}. Materials in the
specular workflow have a specular color instead of a metallic value, so we
show one or the other, followed by the index of refraction.

<<Describe roughness, metallic or specular color, and ior>>=
auto scalarText = [&](const char* label, const ScalarInput& in) {
    if (in.texture >= 0)
        ImGui::TextWrapped("%s: texture channel %c of %s (x%g %+g)", label, "rgba"[in.channel],
                           textures[in.texture].path.c_str(), in.scale, in.bias);
    else
        ImGui::Text("%s: %g", label, in.value);
};
scalarText("Roughness", material.roughness);
if (material.specularWorkflow)
{
    if (material.specularTexture >= 0)
        ImGui::TextWrapped("Specular color: %s", textures[material.specularTexture].path.c_str());
    else
    {
        float color[3] = {material.specularColor[0], material.specularColor[1], material.specularColor[2]};
        ImGui::ColorEdit3("Specular color", color,
                          ImGuiColorEditFlags_NoInputs | ImGuiColorEditFlags_NoPicker);
    }
}
else
    scalarText("Metallic", material.metallic);
ImGui::Text("IOR: %g", material.ior);
@ The last section lists every property of the prim, attributes first and
then relationships, in a table of three columns: name, type and value. The
table takes the rest of the panel's height and scrolls by itself, keeping its
header row in view ([[TableSetupScrollFreeze]]).

<<List the properties of the prim>>=
ImGui::SeparatorText("Properties");
if (ImGui::BeginTable("props", 3,
                      ImGuiTableFlags_RowBg | ImGuiTableFlags_BordersInnerV | ImGuiTableFlags_Resizable |
                          ImGuiTableFlags_ScrollY,
                      ImVec2(0, ImGui::GetContentRegionAvail().y)))
{
    ImGui::TableSetupScrollFreeze(0, 1);
    ImGui::TableSetupColumn("Name", ImGuiTableColumnFlags_WidthStretch, 0.35f);
    ImGui::TableSetupColumn("Type", ImGuiTableColumnFlags_WidthStretch, 0.2f);
    ImGui::TableSetupColumn("Value", ImGuiTableColumnFlags_WidthStretch, 0.45f);
    ImGui::TableHeadersRow();
    <<List the attributes of the prim>>
    <<List the relationships of the prim>>
    ImGui::EndTable();
}
@ An attribute's value is read at the default time. An attribute that has
only time samples has no default value, so for it we show the value at the
earliest sample, and note how many samples there are. The names of attributes
whose value was authored in the file are drawn normally; those that merely
report a fallback value defined by their schema are greyed out.

<<List the attributes of the prim>>=
for (const UsdAttribute& attr : prim.GetAttributes())
{
    VtValue value;
    attr.Get(&value, UsdTimeCode::Default());
    if (value.IsEmpty() && attr.GetNumTimeSamples() > 0)
        attr.Get(&value, UsdTimeCode::EarliestTime());
    ImGui::TableNextRow();
    ImGui::TableNextColumn();
    if (attr.HasAuthoredValue())
        ImGui::TextUnformatted(attr.GetName().GetText());
    else
        ImGui::TextDisabled("%s", attr.GetName().GetText());
    ImGui::TableNextColumn();
    ImGui::TextDisabled("%s", attr.GetTypeName().GetAsToken().GetText());
    ImGui::TableNextColumn();
    ImGui::TextWrapped("%s", valueString(value).c_str());
    if (attr.GetNumTimeSamples() > 0)
    {
        ImGui::SameLine();
        ImGui::TextDisabled("(%zu samples)", attr.GetNumTimeSamples());
    }
}
@ Any USD value can be turned into text by [[TfStringify]], but some values
are long: the points of a mesh may run to megabytes. We cut the text at 160
characters, which is enough to recognize a value and keeps the table
responsive.

<<Format an attribute value for the inspector>>=
std::string valueString(const VtValue& value, size_t maxLength = 160)
{
    std::string s = value.IsEmpty() ? std::string("<no value>") : TfStringify(value);
    if (s.size() > maxLength)
        s = s.substr(0, maxLength) + " ...";
    return s;
}
@ A relationship has no value but a list of target paths, such as the
material bound to a prim.

<<List the relationships of the prim>>=
for (const UsdRelationship& rel : prim.GetRelationships())
{
    SdfPathVector targets;
    rel.GetTargets(&targets);
    std::vector<std::string> names;
    for (const SdfPath& p : targets)
        names.push_back(p.GetString());
    ImGui::TableNextRow();
    ImGui::TableNextColumn();
    ImGui::TextUnformatted(rel.GetName().GetText());
    ImGui::TableNextColumn();
    ImGui::TextDisabled("rel");
    ImGui::TableNextColumn();
    ImGui::TextWrapped("%s", names.empty() ? "<none>" : TfStringJoin(names, ", ").c_str());
}
@* The camera. The orbit camera is described by a target point
$\mathbf t$, a distance $d$, a yaw angle $\theta$ and a pitch angle $\varphi$.
The eye is at $\mathbf e = \mathbf t + d\,\mathbf o$, where the unit vector
$\mathbf o$ points from the target towards the eye. USD stages declare which
axis is ``up'', $Y$ or $Z$, and the camera must follow suit, or a Z-up scene
would lie on its side.

For a Y-up stage, $\theta$ turns around $Y$ starting from the $+Z$ axis, and
$\varphi$ lifts the eye above the $XZ$ plane:
$$\mathbf o = (\cos\varphi\sin\theta,\ \sin\varphi,\ \cos\varphi\cos\theta).$$
For a Z-up stage, $\theta$ turns around $Z$ starting from the $+X$ axis, and
$\varphi$ lifts the eye above the $XY$ plane:
$$\mathbf o = (\cos\varphi\cos\theta,\ \cos\varphi\sin\theta,\ \sin\varphi).$$
In both cases the up vector given to the renderer is simply the world's up
axis; the renderer builds an orthonormal frame from it and the viewing
direction.

A USD camera, when one is chosen, needs no such construction: the scene has
already computed its eye, its directions and its window, and we only fit the
window to the viewport. Either way, this is also the place where all the
settings of the View menu reach the renderer, because it is called whenever
[[m_needsRender]] is set.

<<Send the camera and the options to the renderer>>=
void App::updateCamera()
{
    if (m_camera >= 0)
    {
        <<Send the USD camera to the renderer>>
    }
    else
    {
        <<Send the orbit camera to the renderer>>
    }
    m_renderer.setLightDir(m_light);
    m_renderer.setShadows(m_shadows);
    m_renderer.setMipmaps(m_mipmaps);
    m_renderer.setNormalMaps(m_normalMaps);
    m_renderer.setReflections(m_reflections);
}
@
<<Send the USD camera to the renderer>>=
const SceneCamera& c = m_scene.cameras()[m_camera];
float halfW, halfH;
fittedWindow(c, halfW, halfH);
m_renderer.setCameraView(c.eye.data(), c.forward.data(), c.up.data(), halfW, halfH, c.orthographic,
                         c.clipNear, c.clipFar);
@
<<Send the orbit camera to the renderer>>=
float offset[3], up[3], eye[3];
orbitOffset(offset, up);
for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
    eye[k] = m_target[k] + offset[k] * m_distance;
m_renderer.setCamera(eye, m_target, up, m_fovY);
@ The formulas for $\mathbf o$ and the up axis live in a function of their
own, because the XR output also needs the desktop view's eye and direction.

<<Compute the orbit offset>>=
// Unit vector from the orbit target to the eye, and the world's up axis.
void App::orbitOffset(float offset[3], float up[3]) const
{
    const float cp = std::cos(m_pitch), sp = std::sin(m_pitch), cy = std::cos(m_yaw), sy = std::sin(m_yaw);
    if (m_scene.zUp())
    {
        offset[0] = cp * cy, offset[1] = cp * sy, offset[2] = sp;
        up[0] = 0, up[1] = 0, up[2] = 1;
    }
    else
    {
        offset[0] = cp * sy, offset[1] = sp, offset[2] = cp * cy;
        up[0] = 0, up[1] = 1, up[2] = 0;
    }
}
@ The XR output places the headset where the desktop view's eye is, looking
the same way. For a USD camera these are its own eye and forward direction;
for the orbit camera, the eye is $\mathbf t+d\,\mathbf o$ and the direction
$-\mathbf o$.

<<Find the eye and direction of the desktop view>>=
// Eye and viewing direction of the desktop view, orbit or USD camera.
void App::viewFrame(float eye[3], float forward[3]) const
{
    if (m_camera >= 0)
    {
        const SceneCamera& c = m_scene.cameras()[m_camera];
        std::copy(c.eye.begin(), c.eye.end(), eye);
        std::copy(c.forward.begin(), c.forward.end(), forward);
        return;
    }
    float offset[3], up[3];
    orbitOffset(offset, up);
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
    {
        eye[k] = m_target[k] + offset[k] * m_distance;
        forward[k] = -offset[k];
    }
}
@ A camera's film back rarely has the shape of the viewport. Like usdview's
default ``fit'' policy, we grow the camera's window along one axis until it
has the viewport's aspect ratio $a$: if the viewport is relatively wider than
the window, the window's height is kept and its width becomes $a$ times the
height; otherwise the width is kept. The whole film back thus stays visible,
with extra scene around it, and nothing the camera's author framed is cut off.

<<Fit a USD camera's window to the viewport>>=
// The camera's window grown along one axis to the viewport's aspect ratio, so
// the whole window stays visible (the "fit" conform policy of usdview).
void App::fittedWindow(const SceneCamera& camera, float& halfWidth, float& halfHeight) const
{
    const float aspect = float(m_renderer.width()) / float(std::max(m_renderer.height(), 1u));
    halfWidth = camera.halfWidth;
    halfHeight = camera.halfHeight;
    if (aspect > halfWidth / halfHeight)
        halfWidth = halfHeight * aspect;
    else
        halfHeight = halfWidth / aspect;
}
@ Looking through a camera is only a matter of remembering which one; the
next frame sends it to the renderer.

<<Look through a USD camera>>=
void App::lookThrough(int camera)
{
    m_camera = camera;
    m_needsRender = true;
}
@ A USD camera is fixed by its file, but the user will still want to look
around from it. Any attempt to move the view therefore hands it over to the
orbit camera, which should start exactly where the USD camera was, so that
the image does not jump. The orbit camera needs a target in front of the eye.
We take the point of the line of sight at the depth of the scene's centre,
$\delta=(\mathbf c-\mathbf e)\cdot\mathbf f$, where $\mathbf f$ is the viewing
direction; if the centre is behind the camera, we use the scene's radius (or
1) instead. The orbit offset $\mathbf o$ points from the target to the eye, so
it is $-\mathbf f$, and inverting the formulas of
<<Send the orbit camera to the renderer>> gives the angles: for a Y-up stage
$\varphi=\arcsin(-f_y)$ and $\theta=\operatorname{atan2}(-f_x,-f_z)$; for a
Z-up stage $\varphi=\arcsin(-f_z)$ and $\theta=\operatorname{atan2}(-f_y,-f_x)$.
The field of view is that of the fitted window, $2\arctan h$.

Two things cannot be carried over. The orbit camera has no roll, so a tilted
camera straightens up. And it is always a pinhole: an orthographic view becomes
a perspective one whose distance makes the visible height at the target the
same, $d=h/\tan(\phi/2)$.

<<Hand the view of a USD camera to the orbit camera>>=
// Hands the view over to the orbit camera, which starts where the USD camera
// is, looking the same way. It orbits a point in front of the camera at the
// depth of the scene's center. Roll is lost, and an orthographic camera
// becomes a perspective one that shows the same size at that point.
void App::orbitFromUsdCamera()
{
    if (m_camera < 0)
        return;
    const SceneCamera& c = m_scene.cameras()[m_camera];
    float halfW, halfH;
    fittedWindow(c, halfW, halfH);
    m_camera = -1;
    m_needsRender = true;

    const float* lo = m_scene.boundsMin();
    const float* hi = m_scene.boundsMax();
    const float* f = c.forward.data();
    float depth = 0.0f, radius = 0.0f;
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
    {
        depth += (0.5f * (lo[k] + hi[k]) - c.eye[k]) * f[k];
        radius += (hi[k] - lo[k]) * (hi[k] - lo[k]);
    }
    if (depth < 1e-3f)
        depth = std::max(0.5f * std::sqrt(radius), 1.0f);
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
        m_target[k] = c.eye[k] + f[k] * depth;
    if (c.orthographic)
        m_distance = halfH / std::tan(0.5f * m_fovY * float(M_PI) / 180.0f);
    else
    {
        m_distance = depth;
        m_fovY = 2.0f * std::atan(halfH) * 180.0f / float(M_PI);
    }
    // the orbit offset (from the target to the eye) is -forward
    if (m_scene.zUp())
        m_pitch = std::asin(std::clamp(-f[2], -1.0f, 1.0f)), m_yaw = std::atan2(-f[1], -f[0]);
    else
        m_pitch = std::asin(std::clamp(-f[1], -1.0f, 1.0f)), m_yaw = std::atan2(-f[0], -f[2]);
    m_pitch = std::clamp(m_pitch, -1.55f, 1.55f);
}
@ Framing. To frame an axis-aligned box, we aim at its center and enclose it
in a sphere whose radius $r$ is half the box's diagonal. A sphere of radius
$r$ seen from distance $d$ subtends a half-angle $\alpha$ with
$\sin\alpha = r/d$: the line of sight that grazes the sphere is tangent to it,
and the radius to the point of tangency is perpendicular to that line. The
sphere just fits the vertical field of view $\phi$ when $\alpha = \phi/2$,
that is, when
$$d = {r\over\sin(\phi/2)}.$$
We add 15\% of margin. The viewport is wider than it is tall, so the
horizontal direction then fits as well. A tiny lower bound on $r$ keeps an
empty or flat scene from putting the eye on the target. Framing is done with
the orbit camera, so a USD camera first hands its view over; the framed view
then keeps its direction.

<<Frame a bounding box>>=
void App::frameBounds(const float lo[3], const float hi[3])
{
    orbitFromUsdCamera();
    float radius = 0.0f;
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
    {
        m_target[k] = 0.5f * (lo[k] + hi[k]);
        radius += (hi[k] - lo[k]) * (hi[k] - lo[k]);
    }
    radius = std::max(0.5f * std::sqrt(radius), 1e-3f);
    m_distance = 1.15f * radius / std::sin(0.5f * m_fovY * float(M_PI) / 180.0f);
    m_needsRender = true;
}
@ The scene computes a world-space bounding box for every mesh, and one for
the whole stage, when it reads the geometry. Framing the selection therefore
needs only the selected prim's mesh, if it has one; without a selected mesh
(nothing selected, or a prim such as a transform or a material) we frame the
whole stage.

<<Frame the selection>>=
void App::frameSelection()
{
    const int mesh = m_scene.meshIndex(m_selected);
    if (mesh < 0)
        frameBounds(m_scene.boundsMin(), m_scene.boundsMax());
    else
        frameBounds(m_scene.meshes()[mesh].boundsMin, m_scene.meshes()[mesh].boundsMax);
}
@* The viewport. The middle panel shows the ray-traced image and turns mouse
actions into camera motion and selection. It is also where rendering happens:
the viewport knows its own size, which is the size of the image to render.

<<Draw the viewport>>=
void App::drawViewport()
{
    <<Open the borderless viewport window>>

    <<Cover the viewport with an invisible button>>

    <<Leave a USD camera when the view is moved>>
    <<Orbit with the left mouse button>>
    <<Pan with the right or middle mouse button>>
    <<Zoom with the mouse wheel>>

    <<Compute the resolution of the image>>

    <<Pick the prim under a mouse click>>

    <<Resize the image with the viewport>>
    <<Restart or continue the progressive rendering>>
    <<Show the image in the viewport>>
    ImGui::End();
}
@ The viewport window has no title bar, no scroll bar and no padding, so that
the image fills it to the edges. Without a working renderer, or while the
window is too small to hold an image, it only shows a message (or nothing).

<<Open the borderless viewport window>>=
ImGui::PushStyleVar(ImGuiStyleVar_WindowPadding, ImVec2(0, 0));
ImGui::Begin("Viewport", nullptr, kPanelFlags | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoScrollbar | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoTitleBar);
ImGui::PopStyleVar();

const ImVec2 size = ImGui::GetContentRegionAvail();
const ImVec2 origin = ImGui::GetCursorScreenPos();
if (!m_rendererOk || size.x < 4 || size.y < 4)
{
    ImGui::TextWrapped("%s", m_rendererOk ? "" : "OptiX renderer unavailable.");
    ImGui::End();
    return;
}
@ An image drawn by ImGui does not react to the mouse, and dragging across a
window's empty space would move the window. So we cover the whole viewport
with an invisible button that accepts all three mouse buttons. While the
button is \emph{active}---a mouse button was pressed on it and is still
held---the drag belongs to the viewport, even if the mouse leaves it.

<<Cover the viewport with an invisible button>>=
ImGui::InvisibleButton("viewport", size, ImGuiButtonFlags_MouseButtonLeft | ImGuiButtonFlags_MouseButtonRight |
                                             ImGuiButtonFlags_MouseButtonMiddle);
const ImGuiIO& io = ImGui::GetIO();
const bool hovered = ImGui::IsItemHovered(), active = ImGui::IsItemActive();
@ The three mouse controls below move the orbit camera. If we are looking
through a USD camera, the first drag or wheel step hands the view over to the
orbit camera, before the motion is applied, so the motion continues smoothly
from the USD camera's point of view.

<<Leave a USD camera when the view is moved>>=
// camera controls: left = orbit, right/middle = pan, wheel = zoom; they
// leave a USD camera for the orbit camera
const bool dragging = active && (ImGui::IsMouseDragging(ImGuiMouseButton_Left, 2.0f) ||
                                 ImGui::IsMouseDragging(ImGuiMouseButton_Right, 1.0f) ||
                                 ImGui::IsMouseDragging(ImGuiMouseButton_Middle, 1.0f));
if (dragging || (hovered && io.MouseWheel != 0.0f))
    orbitFromUsdCamera();
@ Dragging with the left button orbits: horizontal motion changes the yaw and
vertical motion the pitch, by 0.006 radians per unit, about a third of a
degree. The pitch is kept within $\pm1.55$ radians, just short of
$\pm\pi/2\approx\pm1.5708$: at exactly a right angle the viewing direction
would be parallel to the up vector, and the camera frame built from their
cross product would collapse. A drag must exceed two units before it counts,
so that a click with a slightly unsteady hand still selects.

<<Orbit with the left mouse button>>=
if (active && ImGui::IsMouseDragging(ImGuiMouseButton_Left, 2.0f))
{
    m_yaw -= io.MouseDelta.x * 0.006f;
    m_pitch = std::clamp(m_pitch + io.MouseDelta.y * 0.006f, -1.55f, 1.55f);
    m_needsRender = true;
}
@ Panning moves the target in the plane of the screen, so that the point under
the mouse follows the mouse. We need the camera's right and up vectors in
world space. With $\mathbf f = -\mathbf o$ the viewing direction and
$\mathbf u_w$ the world's up axis, the right vector is
$\mathbf r \propto \mathbf f\times\mathbf u_w$ and the camera's up vector is
$\mathbf u = \mathbf r\times\mathbf f$. Working out the products with the two
formulas for $\mathbf o$ gives, for a Y-up stage,
$$\mathbf r = (\cos\theta,\ 0,\ -\sin\theta),\qquad
  \mathbf u = (-\sin\varphi\sin\theta,\ \cos\varphi,\ -\sin\varphi\cos\theta),$$
and for a Z-up stage,
$$\mathbf r = (-\sin\theta,\ \cos\theta,\ 0),\qquad
  \mathbf u = (-\sin\varphi\cos\theta,\ -\sin\varphi\sin\theta,\ \cos\varphi).$$

How far should the target move for each unit the mouse moves? At distance $d$,
a vertical field of view $\phi$ spans a height of $2d\tan(\phi/2)$ in the
world, and this height fills the $H$ units of the viewport's height; one unit
of mouse motion is therefore worth $k = 2d\tan(\phi/2)/H$ in the world at the
target's depth. Moving the mouse to the right should drag the scene to the
right, which means moving the target to the left, along $-\mathbf r$; screen
coordinates grow downwards, so moving the mouse down moves the target along
$+\mathbf u$ and the scene down.

<<Pan with the right or middle mouse button>>=
if (active && (ImGui::IsMouseDragging(ImGuiMouseButton_Right, 1.0f) ||
               ImGui::IsMouseDragging(ImGuiMouseButton_Middle, 1.0f)))
{
    // move the target in the camera plane
    const float cp = std::cos(m_pitch), sp = std::sin(m_pitch), cy = std::cos(m_yaw), sy = std::sin(m_yaw);
    float right[3], up[3];
    if (m_scene.zUp())
        right[0] = -sy, right[1] = cy, right[2] = 0, up[0] = -sp * cy, up[1] = -sp * sy, up[2] = cp;
    else
        right[0] = cy, right[1] = 0, right[2] = -sy, up[0] = -sp * sy, up[1] = cp, up[2] = -sp * cy;
    const float k = m_distance * 2.0f * std::tan(0.5f * m_fovY * float(M_PI) / 180.0f) / size.y;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
        m_target[i] += (io.MouseDelta.y * up[i] - io.MouseDelta.x * right[i]) * k;
    m_needsRender = true;
}
@ The wheel zooms by multiplying the distance by $0.88$ per notch forward and
dividing by it per notch backward. A geometric step feels the same at every
scale, from a whole city down to a screw, and the distance can approach the
target but never reach or cross it.

<<Zoom with the mouse wheel>>=
if (hovered && io.MouseWheel != 0.0f)
{
    m_distance *= std::pow(0.88f, io.MouseWheel);
    m_needsRender = true;
}
@ ImGui measures in logical units, but the image should have one ray per
physical pixel. The framebuffer scale converts one into the other. While the
viewport mirrors the headset's left eye, the desktop image is neither rendered
nor picked from: the mirror shows something else.

<<Compute the resolution of the image>>=
const float fbScale = io.DisplayFramebufferScale.x;
const unsigned w = unsigned(size.x * fbScale), h = unsigned(size.y * fbScale);
const bool mirroring = m_xrMirror && m_xr.state() == XrOutput::State::Running && !m_mirrorPixels.empty();
@ Picking. Clicking on the image selects the prim under the mouse. The
renderer already knows which mesh each pixel shows: on the first sample of
every image, which goes through the center of each pixel, it records a mesh
identifier per pixel and copies that buffer back to the CPU. Picking is
therefore a lookup, with no extra ray to trace.

A click is a release of the left button that did not travel far: ImGui
records the largest squared distance the mouse moved while the button was
down, and we accept less than 16, that is, less than 4 units. Anything longer
was an orbit, not a click. We also require that the renderer's buffer has the
viewport's current width, so that pixel coordinates mean the same thing in
both. A mesh id is the mesh's index in the scene, and each mesh records the
path of the prim it came from, so the id leads straight to the prim to
select. The selection is revealed in the tree; a click on the background,
whose id is no mesh's index, clears it.

<<Pick the prim under a mouse click>>=
// pick on click (left release without dragging)
if (hovered && ImGui::IsMouseReleased(ImGuiMouseButton_Left) && io.MouseDragMaxDistanceSqr[0] < 16.0f &&
    m_renderer.width() == w && !mirroring)
{
    const unsigned px = unsigned((io.MousePos.x - origin.x) * fbScale);
    const unsigned py = unsigned((io.MousePos.y - origin.y) * fbScale);
    const unsigned meshId = m_renderer.meshIdAt(px, py);
    select(meshId < m_scene.meshes().size() ? m_scene.meshes()[meshId].path : SdfPath(), true);
}
@ When the viewport changes size, the renderer reallocates its buffers and we
replace the SDL texture by one of the new size. The texture is a
\emph{streaming} texture, meant to be rewritten often, in the pixel format
[[SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA32]], whose bytes are red, green, blue and alpha in
memory order---exactly the layout of the renderer's [[uchar4]] pixels.

<<Resize the image with the viewport>>=
if (w != m_renderer.width() || h != m_renderer.height())
{
    m_renderer.resize(w, h);
    if (m_texture)
        SDL_DestroyTexture(m_texture);
    m_texture = SDL_CreateTexture(m_sdlRenderer, SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA32, SDL_TEXTUREACCESS_STREAMING, int(w), int(h));
    m_needsRender = true;
}
@ Progressive rendering is driven from here. Any change---camera, selection,
settings, a new file, a new size---has set [[m_needsRender]]. We then pass the
new camera and options to the renderer and reset its accumulation, which
throws away the samples of the old image. In every frame, as long as fewer
than [[kMaxSamples]] samples have been taken, the renderer adds one more
sample to each pixel, and the averaged image is copied into the texture. While
the user drags the camera, each frame thus shows a fresh one-sample image;
when the user lets go, the image refines itself in a few seconds and then the
GPU rests.

A failure of the renderer, reported as an exception, disables it and shows the
message in the error popup, rather than ending the program.

<<Restart or continue the progressive rendering>>=
// any change restarts the progressive accumulation; otherwise keep adding
// samples until kMaxSamples (glossy reflections and antialiasing converge)
if (m_needsRender)
{
    updateCamera();
    m_renderer.resetAccumulation();
    m_needsRender = false;
}
if (m_texture && m_rendererOk && m_renderer.samples() < kMaxSamples && !mirroring)
{
    try
    {
        m_renderer.render();
        SDL_UpdateTexture(m_texture, nullptr, m_renderer.pixels().data(), int(w * 4));
    }
    catch (const std::exception& e)
    {
        m_error = std::string("Rendering failed: ") + e.what();
        m_rendererOk = false;
    }
}
@ Finally the texture is drawn over the invisible button. ImGui's SDL renderer
back end interprets a texture identifier as a pointer to an
[[SDL_Texture]]. If the stage has nothing the renderer can draw, a note says so
in the corner, so that an empty viewport is not mistaken for a broken one.
The mirror of the headset, when on, takes the image's place.

<<Show the image in the viewport>>=
if (mirroring)
    drawXrMirror(origin.x, origin.y, size.x, size.y);
else if (m_texture)
    ImGui::GetWindowDrawList()->AddImage(ImTextureRef(ImTextureID(reinterpret_cast<intptr_t>(m_texture))), origin,
                                         ImVec2(origin.x + size.x, origin.y + size.y));
if (m_scene.loaded() && m_scene.meshes().empty())
    ImGui::GetWindowDrawList()->AddText(ImVec2(origin.x + 12, origin.y + 12), IM_COL32(220, 220, 220, 255),
                                        "This stage has no renderable geometry (Mesh, Cube, Sphere).");
@* The status bar and the popups. The status bar is a one-line window at the
bottom. It counts the prims (instance proxies included, the pseudo-root not),
the meshes, the triangles and the textures, as the scene counted them when it
loaded the stage; it names the camera in use, the OptiX version and the GPU,
and shows the progress
of the rendering with the time of the last sample, followed by the state of
the XR output when there is one. [[OPTIX_VERSION]] encodes
version 9.1.0 as 90100, so we take it apart with divisions.

<<Draw the status bar>>=
void App::drawStatusBar()
{
    ImGui::Begin("Status", nullptr, kPanelFlags | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoTitleBar | ImGuiWindowFlags_NoScrollbar);
    ImGui::Text("%s  |  prims: %zu  meshes: %zu  triangles: %zu  textures: %zu  |  camera: %s  |  "
                "OptiX %d.%d.%d on %s  |  samples %u/%u, %.2f ms%s",
                m_scene.loaded() ? m_scene.filePath().c_str() : "no file",
                m_scene.primCount(), m_scene.meshes().size(), m_scene.triangleCount(), m_scene.textures().size(),
                m_camera >= 0 ? m_scene.cameras()[m_camera].path.c_str() : "orbit",
                OPTIX_VERSION / 10000, (OPTIX_VERSION / 100) % 100, OPTIX_VERSION % 100,
                m_renderer.deviceName().c_str(), m_renderer.samples(), kMaxSamples, m_renderer.lastRenderMs(),
                m_xr.state() == XrOutput::State::Off ? "" : ("  |  XR: " + xrStatus()).c_str());
    ImGui::End();
}
@ Two modal popups complete the interface. The error popup opens whenever
[[m_error]] holds a message and stays until the user acknowledges it, which
clears the message. The About box is opened here, at the top level of the
window stack, when the Help menu has raised [[m_showAbout]]; it summarizes
the tools and the mouse and keyboard controls.

<<Draw the popups>>=
void App::drawPopups()
{
    if (!m_error.empty() && !ImGui::IsPopupOpen("Error"))
        ImGui::OpenPopup("Error");
    if (ImGui::BeginPopupModal("Error", nullptr, ImGuiWindowFlags_AlwaysAutoResize))
    {
        ImGui::TextUnformatted(m_error.c_str());
        if (ImGui::Button("OK"))
        {
            m_error.clear();
            ImGui::CloseCurrentPopup();
        }
        ImGui::EndPopup();
    }

    if (m_showAbout)
    {
        ImGui::OpenPopup("About");
        m_showAbout = false;
    }
    if (ImGui::BeginPopupModal("About", nullptr, ImGuiWindowFlags_AlwaysAutoResize))
    {
        ImGui::Text("USD OptiX Viewer");
        ImGui::Separator();
        ImGui::Text("OpenUSD scene graph, meshoptimizer meshes, OptiX %d ray tracing,", OPTIX_VERSION / 10000);
        ImGui::Text("SDL3 windowing and Dear ImGui %s user interface.", IMGUI_VERSION);
        ImGui::Spacing();
        ImGui::TextDisabled("Left drag: orbit   Right/middle drag: pan   Wheel: zoom");
        ImGui::TextDisabled("Click: select   F: frame selection   Esc: clear selection");
        ImGui::TextDisabled("Camera menu: look through a USD camera (moving returns to the orbit camera)");
        ImGui::TextDisabled("XR menu: headset output through OpenXR / NVIDIA CloudXR (--xr, --cloudxr)");
        if (ImGui::Button("Close"))
            ImGui::CloseCurrentPopup();
        ImGui::EndPopup();
    }
}
@* The XR output. The viewer can show its scene in a headset as well: in
stereo, with the head's own motion, through any OpenXR runtime, and in
particular through NVIDIA CloudXR, which streams the images over the network
to an Apple Vision Pro, a Meta Quest or a similar device. The OpenXR side is
the class [[XrOutput]] and the CloudXR side the class [[CloudXrService]], both
described in the next part. Here we see how the application drives them.

<<The XR output of the application>>=
// ---- XR output ----------------------------------------------------------------
<<Start the XR output>>

<<Follow the XR session>>

<<Render the eyes of the headset>>

<<Move the renderer to the GPU of the headset>>

<<Draw the mirror of the left eye>>

<<Describe the state of the XR output>>
@ There are two ways to reach a CloudXR headset. CloudXR may already run as a
service of its own, started for example by NVIDIA's Isaac Teleop tools, which
also set [[XR_RUNTIME_JSON]] so that OpenXR applications find it; then
\texttt{--xr} is enough. Or the viewer runs the CloudXR runtime itself, from
the libraries of a CloudXR SDK, with \texttt{--cloudxr}. The SDK's directory
comes from \texttt{--cloudxr-sdk}, failing that from the environment variable
[[CLOUDXR_SDK_DIR]], and failing that from [[CLOUDXR_SDK_DEFAULT]], a string
literal that the build defines (<<Choose the default CloudXR SDK>>). By
default it names the SDK installed for all users with the other tools,
\lpfile{/opt/viztools/cloudxr-6.2.1}, so that on this machine
\texttt{--cloudxr} alone is enough. The most specific
setting wins: a command line can point at another SDK for one run, and the
environment for a whole shell session. Only a build configured without a
default can end up with no directory at all. Either way the OpenXR instance
is created next; a failure leaves the viewer working on the desktop, with the
reason in the error popup.

<<Start the XR output>>=
// --cloudxr runs the CloudXR runtime in this process; --xr alone uses the
// active OpenXR runtime (XR_RUNTIME_JSON), for instance a CloudXR runtime
// started by Isaac Teleop.
void App::startXr(bool cloudXr, std::string sdkDir, const std::string& profile)
{
    std::string error;
    if (cloudXr)
    {
        // the option, then the environment, then the SDK installed with the tools
        if (sdkDir.empty() && std::getenv("CLOUDXR_SDK_DIR"))
            sdkDir = std::getenv("CLOUDXR_SDK_DIR");
        if (sdkDir.empty())
            sdkDir = CLOUDXR_SDK_DEFAULT;
        if (sdkDir.empty())
        {
            m_error = "--cloudxr needs the CloudXR SDK directory: --cloudxr-sdk DIR or CLOUDXR_SDK_DIR.";
            return;
        }
        if (!m_cloudXr.start(sdkDir, profile, error))
        {
            m_error = "CloudXR: " + error;
            return;
        }
    }
    if (!m_xr.init(error))
    {
        m_error = "XR output unavailable: " + error;
        return;
    }
    m_xrEnabled = true;
}
@ Once per turn of the main loop, the application lets the XR output handle
the runtime's events and then, if a frame is due, renders the two eyes.

A headset is not always there. With CloudXR's \emph{auto} device profiles the
runtime reports no headset until a client connects, which may be minutes
after the program started. When the headset appears, the output reports
[[SystemReady]]: the runtime has chosen a GPU and made a Vulkan device on it.
The eye images are shared between Vulkan and CUDA, which works only on one
and the same GPU, so the renderer moves to that GPU if it is not already
there, and then the session is created.

[[beginFrame]] waits until the headset wants the next frame. That wait paces
the whole loop at the headset's refresh rate, so while the session runs the
desktop must not wait for its own vertical blank as well, or the two clocks
would beat against each other; vsync is switched off and back on as the
session starts and stops. Any error ends the XR output, not the program.

<<Follow the XR session>>=
// Called once per main-loop turn. When the headset appears, the renderer
// moves to the GPU the runtime chose, so that the eye buffers can be shared.
// While the session runs, xrWaitFrame paces the loop at the headset's rate,
// so the desktop stops waiting for vsync.
void App::updateXr()
{
    if (!m_xrEnabled)
        return;
    m_xrClient = m_cloudXr.clientConnected();
    std::string error;
    if (m_xr.update(error) == XrOutput::State::SystemReady && useRendererOn(m_xr.deviceUuid(), error))
        m_xr.createSession(m_xrResolution, error);
    if (m_xr.beginFrame(error))
    {
        const auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        if (m_xr.shouldRender() && m_rendererOk)
            renderXrEyes(error);
        m_xr.endFrame(error);
        m_xrFrameMs = std::chrono::duration<float, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - start).count();
    }
    if (!error.empty())
    {
        m_error = "XR output stopped: " + error;
        m_xr.stop();
        m_xrEnabled = false;
    }
    const bool paced = m_xr.state() == XrOutput::State::Running;
    if (paced != m_xrPaced)
    {
        SDL_SetRenderVSync(m_sdlRenderer, paced ? 0 : 1);
        m_xrPaced = paced;
    }
}
@ Where is the user in the scene? The headset reports the head's pose in its
own space, in meters, relative to where the head was when the session
started. We put that origin at the eye of the desktop view, facing the
horizontal part of its direction (tilting the whole world with the desktop
camera's pitch would be uncomfortable), so the headset starts where the
desktop view is and follows it when the desktop user orbits or picks another
camera. A meter of head motion spans $1/m$ scene units, where $m$ is the
stage's \emph{metersPerUnit}, times the world scale of the XR menu.

Each eye is then rendered by the OptiX renderer straight into its shared
buffer. For the mirror, the left eye is also copied to main memory; this costs
a transfer per frame, which is why the mirror is optional.

<<Render the eyes of the headset>>=
// The XR origin sits at the desktop view's eye, facing its horizontal
// direction, so the headset starts where the desktop camera is and follows it.
void App::renderXrEyes(std::string& error)
{
    XrOutput::Anchor anchor{};
    viewFrame(anchor.eye, anchor.forward);
    anchor.zUp = m_scene.zUp();
    const double metersPerUnit = m_scene.loaded() ? UsdGeomGetStageMetersPerUnit(m_scene.stage()) : 1.0;
    anchor.unitsPerMeter = m_xrWorldScale / float(metersPerUnit);
    try
    {
        for (unsigned int i = 0; i < m_xr.eyeCount(); ++i)
            m_renderer.renderView(m_xr.eyeCamera(i, anchor), m_xr.eyeWidth(), m_xr.eyeHeight(), m_xr.eyeBuffer(i),
                                  unsigned(m_xrSamples));
        if (m_xrMirror)
        {
            m_mirrorPixels.resize(size_t(m_xr.eyeWidth()) * m_xr.eyeHeight());
            if (cudaMemcpy(m_mirrorPixels.data(), m_xr.eyeBuffer(0), m_mirrorPixels.size() * sizeof(uchar4),
                           cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost) != cudaSuccess)
                throw std::runtime_error("copying the mirror image failed");
        }
    }
    catch (const std::exception& e)
    {
        error = std::string("rendering failed: ") + e.what();
    }
}
@ Moving the renderer to another GPU means starting it again there: release
everything, initialize on the GPU with the given UUID, and upload the scene
and the selection anew. The desktop image follows by itself: the renderer's
image size is now zero, so the viewport resizes it on the next frame.

<<Move the renderer to the GPU of the headset>>=
// Moves the renderer to the GPU with the given UUID and uploads the scene again.
bool App::useRendererOn(const std::array<unsigned char, 16>& uuid, std::string& error)
{
    if (m_rendererOk && m_renderer.deviceUuid() == uuid)
        return true;
    m_renderer.release();
    m_rendererOk = m_renderer.init(error, uuid.data());
    if (!m_rendererOk)
        return false;
    if (m_scene.loaded())
        m_renderer.setScene(m_scene);
    const int mesh = m_scene.meshIndex(m_selected);
    m_renderer.setSelectedMesh(mesh >= 0 ? unsigned(mesh) : kNoMesh);
    m_needsRender = true;  // the viewport also resizes the new image
    return true;
}
@ The mirror is an SDL texture of the eye's size, recreated when that size
changes, and drawn as large as fits in the viewport without distortion, on
black.

<<Draw the mirror of the left eye>>=
// The left eye's latest image, fitted inside the viewport.
void App::drawXrMirror(float x, float y, float width, float height)
{
    const int w = int(m_xr.eyeWidth()), h = int(m_xr.eyeHeight());
    if (!m_mirrorTexture || w != m_mirrorWidth || h != m_mirrorHeight)
    {
        if (m_mirrorTexture)
            SDL_DestroyTexture(m_mirrorTexture);
        m_mirrorTexture = SDL_CreateTexture(m_sdlRenderer, SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA32, SDL_TEXTUREACCESS_STREAMING, w, h);
        m_mirrorWidth = w;
        m_mirrorHeight = h;
    }
    if (!m_mirrorTexture || m_mirrorPixels.size() != size_t(w) * h)
        return;
    SDL_UpdateTexture(m_mirrorTexture, nullptr, m_mirrorPixels.data(), w * 4);
    const float scale = std::min(width / float(w), height / float(h));
    const ImVec2 lo(x + 0.5f * (width - w * scale), y + 0.5f * (height - h * scale));
    ImDrawList* list = ImGui::GetWindowDrawList();
    list->AddRectFilled(ImVec2(x, y), ImVec2(x + width, y + height), IM_COL32(0, 0, 0, 255));
    list->AddImage(ImTextureRef(ImTextureID(reinterpret_cast<intptr_t>(m_mirrorTexture))), lo,
                   ImVec2(lo.x + w * scale, lo.y + h * scale));
}
@ Finally, the state of the XR output in words, for the XR menu and the status
bar. While the session runs, it includes the time the last frame took to
render and hand over, not counting the wait for the headset.

<<Describe the state of the XR output>>=
std::string App::xrStatus() const
{
    switch (m_xr.state())
    {
    case XrOutput::State::Off:
        return "off";
    case XrOutput::State::WaitingForSystem:
        return m_cloudXr.running() ? "waiting for a CloudXR client" : "waiting for a headset";
    case XrOutput::State::SystemReady:
    case XrOutput::State::Idle:
        return "headset idle";
    case XrOutput::State::Running:
        break;
    }
    char text[64];
    std::snprintf(text, sizeof(text), "running, %.1f ms per frame", m_xrFrameMs);
    return text;
}
@* Decoding images. Our build of OpenUSD leaves out its imaging libraries,
and with them OpenUSD's image readers, so the scene decodes textures with
Sean Barrett's \lpfile{stb\_image.h}, a single-header library in the public
domain. Such a header contains only declarations, unless a certain macro,
\textit{STB\_\allowbreak IMAGE\_\allowbreak IMPLEMENTATION}, is defined before the header is included; then
it contains the implementation as well. Exactly one source file of the program must
do so, and this is it.

<<UsdOptixViewer/src/StbImage.cpp>>=
// Image decoding for textures (PNG, JPEG, TGA, BMP, HDR, ...).
#define STB_IMAGE_IMPLEMENTATION
#include "third_party/stb_image.h"
